  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186100959,
      "id": "80ed79a9-8e39-5c84-9c87-dec266060afc",
      "title": "Identifying Solutions to First-Order Differential Equations"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "7ec181c2-557d-5feb-b3bd-5a2c975f765a",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/problem": "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2 y}{x},\\,x\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n Which of the following is a solution to this equation?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $y=- 4 x^{3} - 1$\n- **II.** $y=3 x^{2} + 1$\n- **III.** $y=x^{2} + 1$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the stated domain.\n\nWe check each proposed solution by differentiating and substituting it into the original equation. \n\n- Differentiating $y=- 4 x^{3} - 1,$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=- 12 x^{2}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2 y}{x} \\\\ - 12 x^{2} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{2\\cdot \\left(- 4 x^{3} - 1\\right)}{x} \\\\ - 12 x^{2} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{2 \\left(- 4 x^{3} - 1\\right)}{x} \\\\ \\frac{2 \\left(1 - 2 x^{3}\\right)}{x} & \\not\\equiv 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y=- 4 x^{3} - 1$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=3 x^{2} + 1,$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=6 x.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2 y}{x} \\\\ 6 x & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{2\\cdot \\left(3 x^{2} + 1\\right)}{x} \\\\ 6 x & \\overset{?}{=}6 x + \\frac{2}{x} \\\\ - \\frac{2}{x} & \\not\\equiv 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y=3 x^{2} + 1$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=x^{2} + 1,$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=2 x.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2 y}{x} \\\\ 2 x & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{2\\cdot \\left(x^{2} + 1\\right)}{x} \\\\ 2 x & \\overset{?}{=}2 x + \\frac{2}{x} \\\\ - \\frac{2}{x} & \\not\\equiv 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y=x^{2} + 1$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None.\"",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
