    "question": {
      ":question/id": "0bb6502b-7d49-5e59-a4d0-fcc07fff8686",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/problem": "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=- 3 y \\tan{\\left(3 x \\right)},\\,\\cos(3 x)\\ne0.\n$$\n\n Which of the following is a solution to this equation?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $y=- \\cos{\\left(3 x \\right)} - 1$\n- **II.** $y=3 \\cos{\\left(3 x \\right)}$\n- **III.** $y=\\cos{\\left(3 x \\right)}$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the stated domain.\n\nWe check each proposed solution by differentiating and substituting it into the original equation. \n\n- Differentiating $y=- \\cos{\\left(3 x \\right)} - 1,$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=3 \\sin{\\left(3 x \\right)}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =- 3 y \\tan{\\left(3 x \\right)} \\\\ 3 \\sin{\\left(3 x \\right)} & \\overset{?}{=}-3\\cdot \\left(- \\cos{\\left(3 x \\right)} - 1\\right)\\tan\\left(3 x\\right) \\\\ 3 \\sin{\\left(3 x \\right)} & \\overset{?}{=}3 \\sin{\\left(3 x \\right)} + 3 \\tan{\\left(3 x \\right)} \\\\ - 3 \\tan{\\left(3 x \\right)} & \\not\\equiv 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y=- \\cos{\\left(3 x \\right)} - 1$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=3 \\cos{\\left(3 x \\right)},$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=- 9 \\sin{\\left(3 x \\right)}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =- 3 y \\tan{\\left(3 x \\right)} \\\\ - 9 \\sin{\\left(3 x \\right)} & \\overset{?}{=}-3\\cdot \\left(3 \\cos{\\left(3 x \\right)}\\right)\\tan\\left(3 x\\right) \\\\ - 9 \\sin{\\left(3 x \\right)} & =- 9 \\sin{\\left(3 x \\right)}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *true*. Therefore, the function $y=3 \\cos{\\left(3 x \\right)}$ *is* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=\\cos{\\left(3 x \\right)},$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=- 3 \\sin{\\left(3 x \\right)}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =- 3 y \\tan{\\left(3 x \\right)} \\\\ - 3 \\sin{\\left(3 x \\right)} & \\overset{?}{=}-3\\cdot \\left(\\cos{\\left(3 x \\right)}\\right)\\tan\\left(3 x\\right) \\\\ - 3 \\sin{\\left(3 x \\right)} & =- 3 \\sin{\\left(3 x \\right)}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *true*. Therefore, the function $y=\\cos{\\left(3 x \\right)}$ *is* a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
