  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186100959,
      "id": "80ed79a9-8e39-5c84-9c87-dec266060afc",
      "title": "Identifying Solutions to First-Order Differential Equations"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "b4379844-9878-5634-b4ec-661a4e0eec22",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/problem": "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=4 y.\n$$\n\n Which of the following is a solution to this equation?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $y=3 e^{3 x}$\n- **II.** $y=2 - 4 e^{4 x}$\n- **III.** $y=- 3 e^{4 x}$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the stated domain.\n\nWe check each proposed solution by differentiating and substituting it into the original equation. \n\n- Differentiating $y=3 e^{3 x},$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=9 e^{3 x}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =4 y \\\\ 9 e^{3 x} & \\overset{?}{=}4\\cdot \\left(3 e^{3 x}\\right) \\\\ 9 e^{3 x} & \\overset{?}{=}12 e^{3 x} \\\\ - 3 e^{3 x} & \\not\\equiv 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y=3 e^{3 x}$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=2 - 4 e^{4 x},$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=- 16 e^{4 x}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =4 y \\\\ - 16 e^{4 x} & \\overset{?}{=}4\\cdot \\left(2 - 4 e^{4 x}\\right) \\\\ - 16 e^{4 x} & \\overset{?}{=}8 - 16 e^{4 x} \\\\ -8 & \\not\\equiv 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y=2 - 4 e^{4 x}$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=- 3 e^{4 x},$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=- 12 e^{4 x}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =4 y \\\\ - 12 e^{4 x} & \\overset{?}{=}4\\cdot \\left(- 3 e^{4 x}\\right) \\\\ - 12 e^{4 x} & =- 12 e^{4 x}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *true*. Therefore, the function $y=- 3 e^{4 x}$ *is* a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "d0025807-0e34-5085-a865-44b545a3e4c9",
