  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186100959,
      "id": "80ed79a9-8e39-5c84-9c87-dec266060afc",
      "title": "Identifying Solutions to First-Order Differential Equations"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "a14e3a5d-1e72-5ee9-ae40-3b5f1554e7a8",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/problem": "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=3 y \\cot{\\left(3 x \\right)},\\,\\sin(3 x)\\ne0.\n$$\n\n Which of the following is a solution to this equation?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $y=4 \\sin{\\left(3 x \\right)}$\n- **II.** $y=- 3 \\sin{\\left(3 x \\right)}$\n- **III.** $y=2 \\sin{\\left(3 x \\right)} - 2$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the stated domain.\n\nWe check each proposed solution by differentiating and substituting it into the original equation. \n\n- Differentiating $y=4 \\sin{\\left(3 x \\right)},$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=12 \\cos{\\left(3 x \\right)}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =3 y \\cot{\\left(3 x \\right)} \\\\ 12 \\cos{\\left(3 x \\right)} & \\overset{?}{=}3\\cdot \\left(4 \\sin{\\left(3 x \\right)}\\right)\\cot\\left(3 x\\right) \\\\ 12 \\cos{\\left(3 x \\right)} & =12 \\cos{\\left(3 x \\right)}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *true*. Therefore, the function $y=4 \\sin{\\left(3 x \\right)}$ *is* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=- 3 \\sin{\\left(3 x \\right)},$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=- 9 \\cos{\\left(3 x \\right)}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =3 y \\cot{\\left(3 x \\right)} \\\\ - 9 \\cos{\\left(3 x \\right)} & \\overset{?}{=}3\\cdot \\left(- 3 \\sin{\\left(3 x \\right)}\\right)\\cot\\left(3 x\\right) \\\\ - 9 \\cos{\\left(3 x \\right)} & =- 9 \\cos{\\left(3 x \\right)}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *true*. Therefore, the function $y=- 3 \\sin{\\left(3 x \\right)}$ *is* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=2 \\sin{\\left(3 x \\right)} - 2,$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=6 \\cos{\\left(3 x \\right)}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =3 y \\cot{\\left(3 x \\right)} \\\\ 6 \\cos{\\left(3 x \\right)} & \\overset{?}{=}3\\cdot \\left(2 \\sin{\\left(3 x \\right)} - 2\\right)\\cot\\left(3 x\\right) \\\\ 6 \\cos{\\left(3 x \\right)} & \\overset{?}{=}6 \\left(\\sin{\\left(3 x \\right)} - 1\\right) \\cot{\\left(3 x \\right)} \\\\ \\frac{6}{\\tan{\\left(3 x \\right)}} & \\not\\equiv 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y=2 \\sin{\\left(3 x \\right)} - 2$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
