  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186100959,
      "id": "80ed79a9-8e39-5c84-9c87-dec266060afc",
      "title": "Identifying Solutions to First-Order Differential Equations"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "6ed3e280-a5e6-5c0a-96f3-a6f0bf078f96",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/problem": "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=4 y.\n$$\n\n Which of the following is a solution to this equation?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $y=1 - e^{4 x}$\n- **II.** $y=- 4 e^{4 x} - 2$\n- **III.** $y=2 e^{4 x} + 1$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the stated domain.\n\nWe check each proposed solution by differentiating and substituting it into the original equation. \n\n- Differentiating $y=1 - e^{4 x},$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=- 4 e^{4 x}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =4 y \\\\ - 4 e^{4 x} & \\overset{?}{=}4\\cdot \\left(1 - e^{4 x}\\right) \\\\ - 4 e^{4 x} & \\overset{?}{=}4 - 4 e^{4 x} \\\\ -4 & \\not\\equiv 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y=1 - e^{4 x}$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=- 4 e^{4 x} - 2,$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=- 16 e^{4 x}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =4 y \\\\ - 16 e^{4 x} & \\overset{?}{=}4\\cdot \\left(- 4 e^{4 x} - 2\\right) \\\\ - 16 e^{4 x} & \\overset{?}{=}- 16 e^{4 x} - 8 \\\\ 8 & \\not\\equiv 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y=- 4 e^{4 x} - 2$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=2 e^{4 x} + 1,$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=8 e^{4 x}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =4 y \\\\ 8 e^{4 x} & \\overset{?}{=}4\\cdot \\left(2 e^{4 x} + 1\\right) \\\\ 8 e^{4 x} & \\overset{?}{=}8 e^{4 x} + 4 \\\\ -4 & \\not\\equiv 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y=2 e^{4 x} + 1$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None.\"",
