    "question": {
      ":question/id": "5ea5045a-3afb-58ef-a706-703f6bdb8ad7",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/problem": "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=2 y \\cot{\\left(2 x \\right)},\\,\\sin(2 x)\\ne0.\n$$\n\n Which of the following is a solution to this equation?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $y=3 \\sin{\\left(2 x \\right)}$\n- **II.** $y=4 \\sin{\\left(2 x \\right)}$\n- **III.** $y=2 \\sin{\\left(2 x \\right)}$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the stated domain.\n\nWe check each proposed solution by differentiating and substituting it into the original equation. \n\n- Differentiating $y=3 \\sin{\\left(2 x \\right)},$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=6 \\cos{\\left(2 x \\right)}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2 y \\cot{\\left(2 x \\right)} \\\\ 6 \\cos{\\left(2 x \\right)} & \\overset{?}{=}2\\cdot \\left(3 \\sin{\\left(2 x \\right)}\\right)\\cot\\left(2 x\\right) \\\\ 6 \\cos{\\left(2 x \\right)} & =6 \\cos{\\left(2 x \\right)}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *true*. Therefore, the function $y=3 \\sin{\\left(2 x \\right)}$ *is* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=4 \\sin{\\left(2 x \\right)},$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=8 \\cos{\\left(2 x \\right)}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2 y \\cot{\\left(2 x \\right)} \\\\ 8 \\cos{\\left(2 x \\right)} & \\overset{?}{=}2\\cdot \\left(4 \\sin{\\left(2 x \\right)}\\right)\\cot\\left(2 x\\right) \\\\ 8 \\cos{\\left(2 x \\right)} & =8 \\cos{\\left(2 x \\right)}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *true*. Therefore, the function $y=4 \\sin{\\left(2 x \\right)}$ *is* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=2 \\sin{\\left(2 x \\right)},$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=4 \\cos{\\left(2 x \\right)}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2 y \\cot{\\left(2 x \\right)} \\\\ 4 \\cos{\\left(2 x \\right)} & \\overset{?}{=}2\\cdot \\left(2 \\sin{\\left(2 x \\right)}\\right)\\cot\\left(2 x\\right) \\\\ 4 \\cos{\\left(2 x \\right)} & =4 \\cos{\\left(2 x \\right)}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *true*. Therefore, the function $y=2 \\sin{\\left(2 x \\right)}$ *is* a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"",
