  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186100959,
      "id": "80ed79a9-8e39-5c84-9c87-dec266060afc",
      "title": "Identifying Solutions to First-Order Differential Equations"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "d6353126-4224-5a24-8c2e-e99d5a96d599",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/problem": "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=2 y \\cot{\\left(2 x \\right)},\\,\\sin(2 x)\\ne0.\n$$\n\n Which of the following is a solution to this equation?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $y=\\sin{\\left(2 x \\right)} + 2$\n- **II.** $y=1 - \\sin{\\left(2 x \\right)}$\n- **III.** $y=3 \\sin{\\left(2 x \\right)} - 1$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the stated domain.\n\nWe check each proposed solution by differentiating and substituting it into the original equation. \n\n- Differentiating $y=\\sin{\\left(2 x \\right)} + 2,$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=2 \\cos{\\left(2 x \\right)}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2 y \\cot{\\left(2 x \\right)} \\\\ 2 \\cos{\\left(2 x \\right)} & \\overset{?}{=}2\\cdot \\left(\\sin{\\left(2 x \\right)} + 2\\right)\\cot\\left(2 x\\right) \\\\ 2 \\cos{\\left(2 x \\right)} & \\overset{?}{=}2 \\left(\\sin{\\left(2 x \\right)} + 2\\right) \\cot{\\left(2 x \\right)} \\\\ - \\frac{4}{\\tan{\\left(2 x \\right)}} & \\not\\equiv 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y=\\sin{\\left(2 x \\right)} + 2$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=1 - \\sin{\\left(2 x \\right)},$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=- 2 \\cos{\\left(2 x \\right)}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2 y \\cot{\\left(2 x \\right)} \\\\ - 2 \\cos{\\left(2 x \\right)} & \\overset{?}{=}2\\cdot \\left(1 - \\sin{\\left(2 x \\right)}\\right)\\cot\\left(2 x\\right) \\\\ - 2 \\cos{\\left(2 x \\right)} & \\overset{?}{=}2 \\left(1 - \\sin{\\left(2 x \\right)}\\right) \\cot{\\left(2 x \\right)} \\\\ - \\frac{2}{\\tan{\\left(2 x \\right)}} & \\not\\equiv 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y=1 - \\sin{\\left(2 x \\right)}$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=3 \\sin{\\left(2 x \\right)} - 1,$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=6 \\cos{\\left(2 x \\right)}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2 y \\cot{\\left(2 x \\right)} \\\\ 6 \\cos{\\left(2 x \\right)} & \\overset{?}{=}2\\cdot \\left(3 \\sin{\\left(2 x \\right)} - 1\\right)\\cot\\left(2 x\\right) \\\\ 6 \\cos{\\left(2 x \\right)} & \\overset{?}{=}2 \\left(3 \\sin{\\left(2 x \\right)} - 1\\right) \\cot{\\left(2 x \\right)} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\tan{\\left(2 x \\right)}} & \\not\\equiv 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y=3 \\sin{\\left(2 x \\right)} - 1$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None.\"",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
